Text after an atomic inline can wrap away from it, except when it starts
with a character of the GL (other than U+00A0), WJ, or ZWJ line breaking
class, or when the nearest common ancestor of the box and the text
doesn’t wrap. Ladybird decides that with the box standing in the text
before it as U+FFFC OBJECT REPLACEMENT CHARACTER, as Blink does: Its
InlineItemsBuilder puts U+FFFC in the text for each atomic inline, so
UAX #14 decides the breaks around it. Blink still breaks before U+2060
WORD JOINER after an atomic inline, though, where the CSS Text rule and
WPT’s line-breaking-atomic-005 don’t, and Ladybird follows the spec.
